# =============================================================================
# backend/.dockerignore
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# backend/Dockerfile's build context is THIS directory (docker-compose.yml
# uses `build: ./backend`, not the project root), so this file -- not the
# root .dockerignore -- is what keeps local dev cruft out of `COPY . /app/`.
# Without it, anything a developer happens to have on disk locally
# (__pycache__/, a stray .venv/, editor state) would get copied into the
# image and bloat it, and in __pycache__'s case could even ship .pyc files
# built against a different Python version than the image's.
# =============================================================================

**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.venv/
**/venv/
**/*.egg-info/
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/

.git/
.gitignore
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store

*.log

# --- Tests -- CI-only, never needed at runtime, never shipped ---------------
# `pytest backend/tests` (see .github/workflows/ci.yml) always runs against
# the checked-out source tree directly, never against a built image -- there
# is no reason for test code, its fixtures/ (sample CSVs, etc.), or pytest's
# own config to ever cross into `COPY . /app/` in the Dockerfile. Keeping
# these out of the image also shrinks its attack surface (less shipped code
# to audit/scan) and keeps `docker build`/pull faster.
tests/
pytest.ini
